Submitting the form with the FormRoot directive:


- 🔥To submit the form, you can use the FormRoot directive, which automatically listens to the submit
    event and calls the submit action defined with the form() function.
    This submit action:
    1. marks all fields as touched
    2. checks the form validity
    3. if valid, calls your provided async action

- 🔥When defining the form, you can use the second parameter of the form() function to specify the
    submit action:
    login.ts:

        protected readonly loginForm = form(this.credentials, 👉{
            submission: {
                action: async () => await this.userService.authenticate(this.credentials().login,
                this.credentials().password),
                onInvalid: () => this.invalidSubmission.set(true),
                ignoreValidators: 'none'
            }
        })
    
    🔥As you can see, this submission option also allows to define an onInvalid callback, which is called
    when the form is submitted while invalid.

    🔥The ignoreValidators option let you define whether the validation should be ignored when submitting
    the form or not:
        👉 pending (default value) invokes the action even if there are pending asynchronous validations,
            but doesn’t if there are validation errors;
        👉 all invokes the action even if there are validation errors;
        👉 none only invokes the action if there is no error and no pending validation.

    🔥FormRoot receives the form as an input. So, to come back to our example, we can write:
    login.html:

        <form [formRoot]="loginForm">
            <label for="login">Login</label>
            <input id="login" [formField]="loginForm.login" />
            <label for="password">Password</label>
            <input id="password" [formField]="loginForm.password" />
            <button type="submit">Log in</button>
        </form>
    
